course: dictionary
lesson: 1888
title: Names with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Collins is a name, so it is a proper noun. A proper noun names one particular person, place or thing, and it starts with a capital letter. «Mr Collins» tells you which man is meant, and «the Collins Dictionary» tells you which book.
explain: Context tells you which Collins is meant. «On 20 July 1969 the American astronauts Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon in Apollo 11 while Michael Collins circled above.» Michael Collins is a person here, because the sentence tells you he was one of the astronauts.
explain: Look at the words around the name. In «the Collins Dictionary», the word Dictionary tells you Collins is the name of a publisher, not a person. A capital letter alone does not settle it, so always check the clues around the word.
fact: A proper noun is the name of one particular person, place or thing, and it begins with a capital letter.
fact: Collins is a common surname in Ireland, the UK, the USA and Australia.
fact: Collins is also the name of a well-known British publisher of dictionaries and school books.
fact: Michael Collins was an astronaut who flew on Apollo 11 and stayed in orbit while two colleagues landed on the Moon.
fact: A collins, as in a Tom Collins, is a rare word for a tall cold drink for adults, made with lemon juice, sugar and fizzy water.
exercise: «On 20 July 1969 Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon during Apollo 11, while Michael Collins orbited above.» Who is Collins in this sentence? | A kind of spacecraft | A tall cold drink | *An astronaut, a person's surname | A town on the Moon
exercise: «Look it up in the Collins Dictionary.» What does Collins name here? | *The publisher of the dictionary | A type of pen | The Moon | A person who wrote a poem
exercise: Why does Collins start with a capital letter? | Because it is always a drink | Because it always starts a sentence | Because it is a verb for doing something | *Because it is a proper noun, the name of one particular person or thing
test: «Mrs Collins read the story to the class.» Which word is a surname? | *Collins | Mrs | story | class
test: Which fact about Collins is true? | Collins is a country in Europe | Collins is always a planet | *Collins is a common surname in Ireland, the UK, the USA and Australia | Collins is a verb meaning to run fast
exam: Dan thinks Collins must be a place because it has a capital letter. What is the best answer? | *A capital letter shows a proper noun, and Collins can name a person or a publisher | Capital letters always mean a place | Collins is always a town in Ireland | Collins is a verb
